== Combat Terms

*Action:* The main thing you do on your turn. Attack, cast a spell, activate an ability, Dash. One per turn.

*Maneuver:* A secondary resource spent on basic maneuvers (Defend, Shove) or a weapon maneuver (Riposte, Cleave), gated by Disciplines. One per turn.

*Movement:* How far you can move on your turn. Usually 30 ft. Can be broken up before and after your Action.

*Reaction:* A response triggered by someone else's turn. Opportunity attacks, Shield Block, Counterspell. One per round (resets at the start of your turn).

*Free Action:* Anything trivial, talking, dropping an item, drawing a weapon. Unlimited per turn.

*Basic Maneuver:* A combat technique available to everyone. Defend, Disengage, Aid, Shove, Grapple, Command, Catch Breath, Search, Stand Up, Use Item. Costs your Maneuver for the turn.

*Damage Reduction (DR):* Subtracted from incoming physical damage, to a minimum of 1. Your armor grants it by weight class: 1 for light, 2 for medium, 3 for heavy. Talents add to that total, so Iron Skin's +2 stacks with whatever you wear. Your total cannot exceed 3 at Novice, 4 at Adept, or 6 at Master. Shields grant no DR of their own.

*Armor Weight Class:* Armor comes in three weight classes: Light (Padded, Leather, Studded Leather), Medium (Chain Shirt, Breastplate), and Heavy (Half Plate, Chain Mail, Plate). Light suits up in 1 minute, Medium in 5 minutes, Heavy in 10 minutes.

*Damage Tier:* Weak, Standard, or Strong, determines how much damage an attack deals. Ability cards and spells have fixed damage values for each tier. Weapons do not carry damage.

*Effect:* A rider on an ability, spell, or maneuver card that does something beyond damage. Effects resolve by the roll's tier, never by a second roll. One roll resolves both damage and effect. The card text states which tiers carry which effect.

*Finesse:* A weapon property. Use Agility instead of Brawn for attack and damage.

*Versatile:* A weapon property. Can be wielded one- or two-handed (Longsword only). Not the same as the Human ancestry trait named *Versatile*; see *Trait*.

*Reach:* A weapon property. Can attack targets 10 ft away (Spear, Halberd).

*Thrown:* A weapon property. Can be thrown, at a range of 20/60 ft (Dagger, Handaxe, Spear, Throwing Dagger).

*Light:* A weapon property. Can be used as an off-hand weapon for two-weapon fighting (Dagger, Shortsword, Handaxe).

*Loading:* A weapon property. Requires a Maneuver to reload between shots (Crossbow only).

*Two-Handed:* A weapon property. Must be held in both hands and takes 2 slots (Spear, Halberd, Shortbow, Longbow, Crossbow). Compare *Versatile*, which may be used two-handed rather than must.

*Lift X:* An effect that raises the target X feet into the air and holds them there. The target is Restrained while held.

*Push X:* An effect that shoves the target X feet directly away from you.

*Slide X:* An effect that moves the target X feet in a direction you choose.

*Trip:* An effect that knocks the target Prone.

*Protection Value (PV):* A temporary +1 bonus to your defense roll from abilities such as Bastion or Arcane Shield.

*Shield Block:* A reaction that reduces incoming damage by one step: Strong becomes Standard, Standard becomes Weak, and Weak becomes 1. It cannot reduce damage below 1. Requires a shield.

*Opportunity Attack:* A free melee attack when an enemy leaves your reach without Disengaging.

*Concentration:* Some spells require ongoing focus to maintain, and you can hold only one at a time. See *Concentration Spell* in Magic Terms for the check that keeps it.

*Hidden:* Concealed from an observer: attacks against you have Bane, and you are not seen until you attack or the concealment ends.

*Concealment:* Not a condition but the fiction of not being seen: smoke, darkness, undergrowth, a doorway. Concealment is what lets you become Hidden, and heavy concealment is thick enough to hide in without needing anything else.

*Escape:* The Action that frees you from Grappled or Restrained. An Agility roll against the situation rather than the grappler, with the DA setting the difficulty. A Standard result grants a Boon on your next Escape; a Strong result breaks you free and ends the condition.

*Condition:* A temporary status effect. Asleep X, Blinded, Burning X, Charmed, Confused X, Dazed X, Dazzled, Deafened, Frightened, Frozen X, Grappled, Hidden, Incapacitated, Invisible, Marked, Paralyzed, Petrified, Poisoned X, Prone, Restrained, Slowed X, Stunned, Unconscious. Where a condition carries an X, the X is its level, the magnitude the effect sets. The entries below define the conditions that carry a level, and Table 13.3 in @sec-chapter-combat is the full table with what every one of them does and how it ends.

*Asleep X:* Unaware for X minutes. Wakes on taking damage.

*Burning X:* Takes X fire damage per round. Spend an action to end.

*Confused X:* Attacks are made with a Bane; cannot take Reactions.

*Dazed X:* Can take only one action type per turn, for X rounds.

*Dazzled:* Perception checks are made at a Bane, and you cannot benefit from a Boon on sight-based rolls.

*Frozen X:* Incapacitated and takes X cold damage per round. Spend an action to end.

*Marked:* A condition always tied to a specific creature. While Marked by a creature, you gain no benefit from Cover or Concealment against it or its allies, and its attacks against you gain a Boon. If the Mark came from a sworn naming (an oath, a judgment, a public declaration), no one who heard the naming can believe you, and no gift freely given benefits you. The Mark ends when its source says it does, when the marking creature is downed, or when you make amends.

*Petrified:* Turned to stone. Incapacitated, DR +5, immune to non-magical damage.

*Poisoned X:* Takes X poison damage per round and attacks at Bane. Spend an action to end.

*Slowed X:* Speed reduced by X feet; cannot take Reactions.

*Morale Check:* A 3d6 roll (no modifiers) to determine whether an NPC or monster flees, wavers, or stands firm.

*Initiative:* 3d6 + Agility modifier. Determines turn order in combat.

*Surprise:* When one side catches the other unaware. The ambushing side acts first in the opening round, and surprised creatures take a Bane on their first roll. The ambushing side rolls Stealth against a Challenge set by the other side's awareness, and a Standard or better result means the ambush lands.

*Cover:* Terrain that provides protection, in two grades. Half cover gives attackers a Bane on their roll against you. Full cover gives them triple Bane.

*Non-Lethal Attack:* A melee attack that reduces a creature to 0 HP but leaves them unconscious and stable instead of dying.

*Grit:* The number of times you can be dropped to 0 HP and get back up before you fall: 2 at Novice, 3 at Adept, 4 at Master. When your HP reaches 0, spend one Grit to reset to maximum HP and roll on the Wound Table. Returns on a respite, a full night's rest.

*Dying:* At 0 HP, you fall Unconscious and roll 3d6 each turn to determine if you stabilize, worsen, or die.

*Wounded:* Below half your maximum HP. See *Wound* for what happens when you are dropped.

*Wound:* A lasting injury from combat. When you spend a point of Grit, you gain a Wound and roll on the Wound Table (see @sec-chapter-combat). Wounds linger after HP is restored and heal one per long rest (a Standard Medicine check during a rest heals one additional wound).

*Thunder:* A damage type: concussive sound and shockwaves. Part of the Elemental family alongside Fire, Cold, Lightning, Acid, and Poison.

*Resistance:* Halves damage of a specific type, round down. A creature resistant to Fire takes half damage from fire attacks.

*Vulnerability:* Doubles damage of a specific type. A creature vulnerable to Slashing takes double damage from slashing attacks.

